WEBVTT

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:15.320
When Akagi and Ichikawa first 
started their zetsu ichimon game,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.820
they appeared evenly matched,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.560
but now it is becoming 
clear who is superior.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.150
Ichikawa now has more 
than 100,000 points.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:29.340
On the other hand, Akagi is steadily 
losing points and now stands at 23,000.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.070
Ichikawa takes that opportunity 
to change his play style.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.160
He gives up on tsumo wins

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.040
and focuses on getting direct 
hit wins off of Akagi.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.570
He's going in for the kill.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:46.320
<b>Episode 6</b>

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:46.330
<b>The Picaro's Talent</b>

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:51.190
<i>We've reached the first turning point.</i>

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.190
<i>Will Ichikawa deal a finishing blow, 
or will Akagi make a comeback?</i>

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.430
<i>They're both going for a one suit hand?</i>

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.430
<i>I see, I see.</i>

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.470
<i>It's not zetsu ichimon anymore; 
it's zetsu nimon, eliminating two suits.</i>

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:09.440
<i>It'll be interesting to watch them 
both go for a characters chinitsu.</i>

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:13.200
If zetsu ichimon is like boxing's in-fighting,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.940
then zetsu nimon is like grappling.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.700
It's as if they have their hands 
on each other's throats

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.950
and are slowly tightening their grips.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.250
The one who can't hold out any longer 
and coughs up the ron tile first dies.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.690
<i>Akagi, isn't that a little reckless?</i>

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.990
<i>Luck isn't on your side right now.</i>

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.220
<i>Ichikawa's much more likely 
to get his winning tile</i>

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.000
<i>than you are to get yours.</i>

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.710
Yasuoka's hypothesis was correct.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:43.500
Akagi has no momentum right now. 
Everything is in Ichikawa's favor.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.740
The seventh turn.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:50.780
Ichikawa is in tenpai.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.240
He's waiting for a 3, 6, or 9 of characters.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.250
<i>Looks like Ichikawa might be the winner...</i>

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.290
<i>He's in tenpai with a concealed 
dora triplet of red dragons</i>

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.450
<i>and an ideal three-way wait, 
so early in the game.</i>

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.860
<i>Akagi's miles away.</i>

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:05.760
Kan!

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:08.990
The new dora is the 6 of characters.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:10.600
Riichi!

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:12.620
In the same round,

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.530
not just the dealer, but the 
South player is also in tenpai.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.360
They've caught up with no delay.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.220
It looks as though the match 
may end here in the seventh turn.

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:23.360
But...

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.500
Just then, Akagi's hand 
is still at this stage.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.640
You may say he's one tile 
away from a chiitoitsu,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.500
but the 9 of characters that he must

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.950
discard for that happens to 
be Ichikawa's winning tile.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.580
Akagi senses that this tile is dangerous.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:38.620
And then...

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:38.960
Chi.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.630
With this call, he manages to secure one 
9 of characters tile in a sequence.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:48.970
This way, he's not likely to have to discard 
the remaining 6 or 9 of characters.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:53.140
Akagi's sharp insight shines as this 
remarkable play gets him out of danger.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:56.280
But Ichikawa is also privy to this strategy.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.440
He can read Akagi's moves precisely.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.920
<i>He won't discard the 3, 6, or 9 now.</i>

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:04.410
5 of characters.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:05.420
Chi.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:09.710
Now Ichikawa's waiting for 
a 2, 5 or 8 of characters.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.060
He changes his wait to the 8 of characters

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.820
that Akagi is most likely to discard next.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:15.410
He chases him down.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.920
As his target moves, so does he.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.930
Like a homing missile.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:23.530
7 of characters!

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.170
Chi.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.170
With that move, he's used up 
his 8 of characters,

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:28.740
and he discards the 9 of characters.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.680
He's sidestepped Ichikawa's 
waits once again.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:34.680
The ninth turn.

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:36.680
The South player deals the 2 of characters.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.180
This is one of Ichikawa's winning tiles.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.320
Yet he doesn't move.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.580
He will hold out for a 
direct hit off of Akagi.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:45.920
Pon.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.160
With this call, Akagi has 
now caught up to Ichikawa.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:53.160
He managed to worm out of Ichikawa's 
waits and get into tenpai himself.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.900
He's waiting on the 
1 and 4 of characters.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:00.060
Now Ichikawa faces another decision.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.950
<i>Akagi took care of his 
3, 6, and 9 of characters,</i>

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.640
<i>as well as his 2, 5, and 8 of characters.</i>

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:10.380
Therefore, all that's left in his hand 
now are 1, 4 and 7 of characters.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.050
<i>Our waits are the same.</i>

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.240
Ichikawa's waiting on the 
1, 4 and 7 of characters.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.380
He chases Akagi for the third time.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:19.740
Akagi waits for the 1 and 4 of characters.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.090
The waits are similar,

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.900
but Ichikawa has a slight 
advantage since his wait

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.280
also includes the 7 of characters.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.070
Akagi's drawn the worst tile, 
the 7 of characters dora.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.770
<i>Looks like you got it.</i>

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:40.010
4 of characters.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:41.740
Ron!

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.610
Dealer haneman. 18,000.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.120
<i>I finally got you, Akagi.</i>

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.730
We're dead.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.520
I guess Ichikawa's too much for him...

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.800
I wouldn't say that.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.090
Indeed, Ichikawa's persistence 
as he hunted down

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:09.300
Akagi's tiles made for an excellent game.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:12.250
His tenacity and intuition are superhuman.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:15.370
However, Akagi's game was 
not worse, by any means.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:19.310
In fact, in terms of impressiveness, 
his game may have surpassed Ichikawa's.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.940
What exactly do you mean?

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.150
This last hand, Akagi consistently 
dodged Ichikawa's waits,

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:32.550
and finally managed to get himself 
into a 1 or 4 of characters wait tenpai.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:36.190
If you had drawn the 7 of characters 
then, what would you have done?

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.390
Huh? Well...

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.960
Figuring that the 1 and 4 of 
characters are his winning tiles,

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.880
wouldn't you have discarded the 7?

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.080
Most people would try to 
preserve their tenpai,

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.210
hoping for that tiny chance of it passing.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:55.210
But Akagi didn't try to pursue 
that small chance.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.230
If he'd discarded the 7 of characters dora,

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.590
Ichikawa would've had a baiman 
and Akagi would've gone bust.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.620
His tenacity is truly astonishing.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:09.220
Would a normal person 
even consider all that?

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:10.690
And even if they considered it,

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:13.740
would they be able to resist the 
temptation to maintain their tenpai?

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.680
Akagi did just that!

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:20.940
Chasing after something as ephemeral 
as "if" will never bring you victory.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:26.940
I can hear Akagi's will, clear as any voice, 
from the 7 of characters he kept.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.470
Akagi...

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.920
Winning is something that happens in reality.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.790
Without pursuing reality, 
there is no way to win.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.600
Akagi dealt into someone's 
hand, but left his will behind.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.310
The will to win in reality.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.590
The game is not favoring Akagi right now.

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:50.600
He may have limited the loss to a haneman,

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:53.680
but drawing the winning 
tile itself was bad luck.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:55.600
It's obvious that the game 
is favoring Ichikawa.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.000
Akagi has 5,000 points, 
and that's all he has.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.610
It may just end here.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:03.610
<i>I'm sorry, Akagi.</i>

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:07.620
<i>I understand that this player 
is out of your league.</i>

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:12.320
<i>But you've always overturned 
accepted notions like those.</i>

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:14.120
<i>You're a genius, no questioning that.</i>

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:15.770
<i>Please...</i>

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.820
<i>show me one last time...</i>

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.890
<i>an escape from this dilemma.</i>

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:21.500
<i>We need some miracle tiles!</i>

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:23.840
<i>Akagi!</i>

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:26.960
<i>What a crap hand!</i>

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.370
<i>He might as well keep the terminal and 
honor tiles and go for a kokushi musou.</i>

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.370
Kokushi musou: Yakuman hand with one of each terminal 
and honor tile, plus one tile that matches any of the other 13.

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:35.070
<i>The luck just isn't on his side right now.</i>

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:36.380
<i>The luck...</i>

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:39.880
<i>I knew it.</i>

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:41.970
<i>There's no way we can expect Akagi</i>

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:43.810
<i>to get a decent hand 
under these circumstances.</i>

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:52.460
<i>That's an amazing hand.</i>

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:54.140
<i>Two dora to boot.</i>

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.370
<i>With a tsumo win, he could 
get up to a haneman.</i>

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:59.490
<i>And he's only two tiles 
away from tenpai already.</i>

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:02.900
<i>It's the complete opposite 
of Akagi's starting tiles.</i>

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.230
<i>Who could've predicted 
such a huge difference?</i>

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:06.980
<i>If Ichikawa wins with a haneman,</i>

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.340
<i>he'll get 6,000 from each 
player as the dealer.</i>

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:13.050
<i>Akagi will have to give up his 
last 5,000 points and die.</i>

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:15.550
<i>And that will be the end.</i>

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.820
<i>Damn it!</i>

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:20.060
<i>Even his draws are complete shit.</i>

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:22.020
<i>It's the sixth turn and he's still got nothing!</i>

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:25.220
I guess there's nothing he can do...

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:26.080
No.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:27.430
He's on it already.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:28.330
Huh?

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:33.630
Looking at Akagi's discard pile...

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:35.630
<i>That's it!</i>

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:38.140
<i>I see what he's getting at...</i>

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:42.410
<i>He's hinting at a kokushi musou 
or a chinitsu to tie their hands.</i>

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:43.390
Riichi!

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:46.480
<i>It's hopeless.</i>

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.380
<i>No one sees him as a threat right now.</i>

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:51.890
<i>His attempt was totally ineffective.</i>

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.890
<i>His final effort failed. Is this the end?</i>

00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.630
<i>5 of characters?</i>

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:00.490
<i>That's right by the dora!</i>

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:02.990
<i>Have you gone insane?!</i>

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:06.330
Dealing into another player's 
hand right now is fatal,

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:07.230
yet Akagi makes a bold move.

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:09.330
And again on the next turn...

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:14.840
He discards a 4 of characters dora, 
making two consecutive reckless moves.

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:17.340
<i>This is crazy.</i>

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:19.790
<i>I get it.</i>

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:23.450
<i>Akagi's betting his life on this bluff.</i>

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:26.580
These character tiles are exactly 
what the player in riichi wants.

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:30.930
Akagi was ready to face death when 
he discarded those two tiles.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.320
His hand is in shambles.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:37.440
At first, it seems foolish to bet 
his life on a trash hand like this.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:41.830
However, that's precisely what is 
needed in order to deceive others.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:45.300
People are always disoriented by 
actions that seem counterproductive.

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:49.290
How can you ever fool anyone 
without risking anything yourself?

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:52.850
Akagi's boldness plants 
doubt in his opponents.

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:57.520
Doubt gives way to delusions, 
and delusions give birth to fear.

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:02.340
To them, his trash hand no 
longer looks like a trash hand.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:06.660
With this one discard,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.590
Akagi's bluff now controls the entire table.

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:11.620
The next player gives up on winning.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:14.350
The player before him looks troubled, as well.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:18.640
And once you've gained 
control, lies becomes truths.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:21.800
Akagi arrives at his own truth by 
deliberately weakening himself.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:25.770
Ichikawa is in tenpai.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:27.430
<i>This is perfect.</i>

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:28.660
<i>If he discards the 5 of characters,</i>

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:30.150
<i>he's looking at a haneman tsumo.</i>

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:32.170
<i>He can end the match—</i>

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:33.150
<i>What?!</i>

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:36.650
R-Ron!

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:39.090
<i>What?! Why?!</i>

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:40.150
<i>It can't be!</i>

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:42.030
<i>I see.</i>

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:45.350
<i>A judgment typical of a man 
as pragmatic as Ichikawa.</i>

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:49.480
<i>Right now, the only thing Ichikawa 
wants to prevent is Akagi winning.</i>

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:54.060
<i>He probably knew the kokushi musou
had a 90% chance of being a bluff,</i>

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.220
<i>but he figured,</i>

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:57.950
<i>on the off-chance that Akagi 
escapes out of reach again,</i>

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:02.230
<i>he'd be better off throwing away this hand.</i>

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:08.720
<i>For Ichikawa, dealing into that 
hand was the logical conclusion.</i>

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:14.350
<i>But this sort of underhanded play 
might change the flow of the game.</i>

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:18.280
<i>Thinking about it, 
this was a godly play by Akagi.</i>

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:21.730
<i>He used his terrible 
starting hand and his draws</i>

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:24.930
<i>to force Ichikawa out of the dealer seat.</i>

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:26.800
<i>With this trash hand...</i>

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:32.310
To rewrite lies into truths, 
he bled himself without hesitation.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:36.120
Each of Akagi's tiles seems to 
glisten with his own blood,

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.310
placing unsurmountable 
pressure on Ichikawa.

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:42.650
<i>This is Akagi's Mahjong...</i>

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:48.990
Akagi's just come back 
from the depths of Hell,

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:53.120
and his starting hand looks like 
he's just made a pact with the devil.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:56.330
It's an extraordinary starting hand.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:58.000
Could this be the luck 
Akagi was born with?

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:00.330
<i>Whenever he survives 
a near-death encounter,</i>

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.910
<i>Akagi returns with a monster hand.</i>

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:04.840
Then, five turns later...

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:06.070
Tsumo.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:08.990
He wins this unbelievable 
hand with a tsumo.

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:12.580
Honitsu, tsumo, chanta, san ankou, 
red dragons, green dragons, 1 dora.

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:15.080
A sanbaiman.

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:15.080
Sanbaiman - Triple mangan.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:19.080
The sand at the depths 
of Hell is magical sand.

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:24.170
Touching it will reverse the poles 
of great fortune and great loss,

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:26.590
much like a coin turning 
from one side to another.

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:29.830
Now Akagi is the dealer.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:32.830
He takes another 5,800 
from the player after him.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.330
Then he gets another dealer 
mangan with a tsumo win.

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:39.640
He gives himself up to his flow 
and goes on a winning streak.

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:42.840
He now has 47,000 points.

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:46.090
He's made it back to just under 50,000.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.530
But the problem is still ahead.

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:54.530
Ichikawa is a wall he cannot penetrate.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:58.460
He was unable to get a direct win 
off the real target, Ichikawa.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:13:59.900
In this game,

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:04.030
he must decrease Ichikawa's points 
if he wants to approach victory.

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:08.640
Akagi attempts unorthodox waits, 
hell waits, and all other manners of waits,

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:11.470
but Ichikawa plays too defensively 
and never deals into his hand.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:12.970
Riichi.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:16.210
Akagi doggedly tries to 
win off of Ichikawa.

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:20.210
He goes into a hell wait for 
the last of the four wind tiles.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:23.950
The game, however, ends up 
being another fruitless draw.

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:25.830
Ichikawa will not deal into his hand.

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:29.770
Oh, boy.

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.190
Let's stop this.

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:35.420
It's never going to end, Ichikawa-san.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:41.590
What good does it do for us to 
keep piling up these points?

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:43.200
What we really want

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.800
are the points that we have to 
pay with our flesh and bone.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:49.040
What are you trying to say?

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:52.130
Let's cut it down to one tenth.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.460
Right now, I have 50,000 and 
you have 100,000 points.

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:59.550
Let's cut these down to 5,000 
and 10,000 and start over.

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:02.770
What?!

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:06.260
You just managed to get yourself up 
to 50,000 points and into the clear!

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:08.160
Why would you do something that risky?

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.060
No, this could actually be interesting.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.960
Ichikawa has 100,000 points.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:16.560
It would be basically 
impossible to make him bust.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:19.570
Looking at it that way, 
it's not a bad idea to gamble.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:23.800
The bigger problem is getting 
Ichikawa to accept this term.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:26.850
I refuse.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:33.050
I would refuse even if you 
had as many points as I do.

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:38.570
I won't do anything that would lower my 
chances of winning by even a single percent.

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:42.820
Fine, then. You stubborn old fart.

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:46.280
Looks like I'll have to force the issue.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:51.830
<i>I can tell this kid's up to no good again.</i>

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:55.180
<i>What's he going to do now?</i>

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:58.800
Akagi is still on his dealer streak.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.080
As always, the game flows in his favor.

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:04.810
On the ninth turn, he's in tenpai.

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:08.600
<i>He's got two choices.</i>

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:12.780
<i>He could go for the south wind 
hell wait, or 6 or 9 of characters.</i>

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:17.440
<i>But he's already confirmed that 
single hell waits aren't effective.</i>

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.830
<i>And his discards are a dead 
giveaway that he's after a honitsu.</i>

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:23.830
<i>A south wind wait would be meaningless.</i>

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:29.220
<i>It's nothing special, but right 
now, he's got no other option.</i>

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:30.340
Riichi!

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:35.670
Then, on Akagi's turn...

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:37.800
He doesn't chase it.

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:39.410
He simply discards the tile he drew.

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.200
In the 12th turn, the player before him

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:43.150
discards a 6 of characters.

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:45.650
This is Akagi's winning tile.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:48.460
However, Akagi does not win.

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:48.890
Chi.

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:50.390
He makes his move.

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:54.040
He calls the 6 of characters 
and discards the 9,

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:56.130
switching his winning tile, 
but giving up a ron win.

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.020
Initially, this call seems meaningless,

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:00.850
but it marks the beginning of 
Akagi's hunt for Ichikawa...

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:02.520
Hold it.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:03.630
You can't do that.

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:04.880
Huh?

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:06.650
You can't call a 6 of 
characters and discard a 9.

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:08.820
We don't accept switch-ups like those.

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:09.680
Put it back.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:13.640
Akagi is uncharacteristically accommodating.

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:15.380
He puts the discarded 9

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:17.880
and the revealed 7 and 8 
back into his hand,

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:20.890
and returns the 6 he had 
called to the discard pile.

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:22.880
He then draws a new tile...

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:24.850
and discards that tile immediately.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.000
<i>I get it.</i>

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:31.160
<i>Now I see what he's getting at.</i>

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:33.310
<i>He only pretended to not 
know about that rule,</i>

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:35.580
<i>and that in itself is the trap.</i>

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:38.900
<i>Since he can still call on tiles, 
it means he's not in tenpai.</i>

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:44.400
<i>The 6 he called and the 9 he 
discarded, at least, will seem safe.</i>

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:47.440
<i>He wanted to emphasize 
that these tiles are safe</i>

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:51.710
<i>and something this elaborate would 
be enough to fool most people.</i>

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:55.390
<i>Probably even Ichikawa is no exception.</i>

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:56.810
No.

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:01.160
Ichikawa saw through it.

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:07.070
<i>You're a sly little monkey who 
likes to use dirty tricks.</i>

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:10.670
<i>But that trick's been 
around for a long time.</i>

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:13.930
<i>I can't discard this 9 of characters.</i>

00:18:13.930 --> 00:18:16.130
<i>It's the most dangerous tile.</i>

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:17.850
<i>Which means that...</i>

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:20.320
<i>The safest tile is...</i>

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:25.270
<i>The west wind.</i>

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:27.180
<i>The fourth west wind.</i>

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:29.310
<i>This is 100% safe.</i>

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:31.190
<i>I have to stay on the defensive.</i>

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:38.190
That's it, old man.

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:43.740
<i>I-Impossible...</i>

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:45.900
<i>This was supposed to 
be the fourth west wind.</i>

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:47.630
<i>That can't happen!</i>

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:50.700
<i>Is it really a west wind?!</i>

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:52.070
Y-Yes!

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:58.690
<i>H-How did he...</i>

00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:02.420
<i>I'm sitting right behind him 
and I still don't understand.</i>

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:05.550
<i>Until a second ago, he was 
waiting on a 6 and 9 of characters.</i>

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:08.300
<i>That's it!</i>

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:10.690
<i>He switched the west wind 
with the 9 of characters!</i>

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:13.930
<i>He filched it after that call.</i>

00:19:13.930 --> 00:19:16.170
<i>When he was returning the tiles.</i>

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:21.230
<i>He's insane!</i>

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:23.740
<i>Is this what he meant 
by forcing the issue?!</i>

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:27.740
<i>I can't believe this guy...</i>

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:30.210
<i>It's not possible.</i>

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:33.670
<i>I dealt the fourth west wind.</i>

00:19:33.670 --> 00:19:36.920
<i>Unless he had a kokushi musou, 
that tile was 100% safe.</i>

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:41.230
<i>Which means he pulled that tile 
from somewhere in the discard pile.</i>

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:44.920
<i>He got me.</i>

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:48.250
<i>We have no way to stop 
him from doing this.</i>

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:51.220
<i>The longer the match goes on, 
the worse my position gets.</i>

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:53.000
<i>So that's his game.</i>

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:55.880
What do you say, Ichikawa-san?

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:57.610
Will you accept my proposal?

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:01.800
Fine.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:03.240
I accept.

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:05.620
Ichikawa...

00:20:05.620 --> 00:20:11.250
<i>I'll make you regret cheating 
against me... Akagi.</i>

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:16.990
That's the spirit. I knew you'd 
understand, Ichikawa-san.

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:22.940
<i>He got his way in the end.</i>

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:24.490
<i>He really is ruthless.</i>

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:26.790
<i>At this point, I can't even be appalled.</i>

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:28.340
<i>I can only admire his natural talent</i>

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:31.030
<i>that allowed him to execute 
this so masterfully.</i>

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:34.180
The points are cut to one tenth.

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:38.200
Akagi goes from 68,000 to 6,800.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:42.330
Ichikawa goes from 81,700 to 8,100.

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:44.450
They are now both under 10,000 points.

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:49.290
The game can now be settled in one blow.

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:53.910
It was Akagi's talent that 
created these conditions.

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:55.490
That's right.

00:20:55.490 --> 00:21:01.560
Akagi's true nature, 
which is undoubtedly evil.

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:06.380
Evil of the highest and purest grade.

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:08.210
A picaro.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>Next Episode</b>

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:20.550
<b>An Innocent Devil</b>

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:19.790
Next time: "An Innocent Devil."
